<!-- 3 SERVICES -->
<section class="light" id="services"><div class="in">
 <p class="eb">What we build and run</p>
 <h2>Seven remodeling marketing services, one connected system</h2>
 <p class="lead">An engine with six strong parts and one weak one runs at the level of the weak one. The <a href="/6-gap-revenue-map/">6-Gap Revenue Map™</a> shows which part is costing you most, and we start there, often on the inquiries you already have.</p>
 <div class="tlax" aria-hidden="true"><span>Marketing that brings the inquiry</span><span>Sales systems that book the project</span></div>
 <ol class="tline">
  <li><h3 class="tb"><button type="button" id="svb1" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="svp1"><span class="tn">01</span><span class="tw">Build demand for the projects you want to win</span><span class="th">Google Ads and Local Services Ads</span></button></h3><div class="tp" id="svp1" role="region" aria-labelledby="svb1"><p class="tph">How we fix it</p><p class="tpw">01 · Build demand for the projects you want to win.</p><p>Google Ads and Local Services Ads for remodelers, filtered to homeowners ready to remodel, not DIYers pricing a backsplash, and judged on cost per booked project. We evaluate demand, close rates, and profitability by project type, so your budget goes toward the work that makes the most business sense. Meta ads and retargeting stay in front of homeowners during weeks of research, and direct mail reaches the neighborhoods where you already build. <a class="tl" href="/ppc-for-remodeling-contractors/">Remodeling PPC <span class="ar">→</span></a></p><p class="when"><b>When it pays:</b> 2–4 weeks, in new consult requests.</p></div></li>
  <li><h3 class="tb"><button type="button" id="svb2" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="svp2"><span class="tn">02</span><span class="tw">Get found where homeowners plan the remodel</span><span class="th">Local SEO and Google Maps</span></button></h3><div class="tp" id="svp2" role="region" aria-labelledby="svb2" data-closed><p class="tph">How we fix it</p><p class="tpw">02 · Get found where homeowners plan the remodel</p><p>Local SEO for remodeling contractors: a Google Business Profile that wins the map pack, then project-type pages for "kitchen remodeler in [city]," "bathroom remodel near me," "home addition contractor" and every town you build in, with reviews by project type. <a class="tl" href="/seo-for-remodeling-contractors/">Remodeling SEO <span class="ar">→</span></a></p><p class="when"><b>When it pays:</b> 3–6 months, then compounds for years.</p></div></li>
  <li><h3 class="tb"><button type="button" id="svb3" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="svp3"><span class="tn">03</span><span class="tw">Own your lead source</span><span class="th">Exclusive inquiries you own</span></button></h3><div class="tp" id="svp3" role="region" aria-labelledby="svb3" data-closed><p class="tph">How we fix it</p><p class="tpw">03 · Own your lead source</p><p>We build exclusive remodeling lead sources you control, bringing remodeling inquiries directly to your business. We also use providers like Angi and HomeAdvisor when their leads produce profitable projects. <a class="tl" href="/remodeling-contractor-lead-generation/">Remodeling lead generation <span class="ar">→</span></a></p><p class="when"><b>When it pays:</b> as the mix shifts, with close rates in a different class.</p></div></li>
  <li><h3 class="tb"><button type="button" id="svb4" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="svp4"><span class="tn">04</span><span class="tw">Get chosen before the first call</span><span class="th">A website that books the consult</span></button></h3><div class="tp" id="svp4" role="region" aria-labelledby="svb4" data-closed><p class="tph">How we fix it</p><p class="tpw">04 · Get chosen before the first call</p><p>A remodeling website that shows your work by neighborhood and project type, recent reviews, and a clear next step above the fold. A researching homeowner should finish your site wanting the consult.</p><p class="when"><b>When it pays:</b> 4–8 weeks, across every channel.</p></div></li>
  <li><h3 class="tb"><button type="button" id="svb5" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="svp5"><span class="tn">05</span><span class="tw">Win the comparison</span><span class="th">Reviews and referrals</span></button></h3><div class="tp" id="svp5" role="region" aria-labelledby="svb5" data-closed><p class="tph">How we fix it</p><p class="tpw">05 · Win the comparison</p><p>Homeowners choose a remodeling contractor on more than price. They're trusting you with their home, often for weeks or months. We help your team request reviews after every completed project, organize them by project type, and respond to each one. We also build in referral requests while homeowners are still excited to show off their finished space. <a class="tl" href="/services/reputation-management-for-contractors/">Reputation <span class="ar">→</span></a></p><p class="when"><b>When it pays:</b> 2–3 months, then every month after.</p></div></li>
  <li><h3 class="tb"><button type="button" id="svb6" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="svp6"><span class="tn">06</span><span class="tw">Win the consult and carry it to a signature</span><span class="th">Speed to lead and proposal follow-up</span></button></h3><div class="tp" id="svp6" role="region" aria-labelledby="svb6" data-closed><p class="tph">How we fix it</p><p class="tpw">06 · Win the consult and carry it to a signature</p><p>A new inquiry reached in under five minutes, the consult booked and confirmed, no-shows reduced, then the proposal followed until it is a yes or a no. A proposal can sit for weeks before a decision is made. We track open proposals and follow-up so your team knows which opportunities need attention. For design-build firms, the paid design agreement and the construction contract are tracked as separate steps. It lifts every channel above at once. <a class="tl" href="/services/lead-to-booked-job/">How it works <span class="ar">→</span></a></p><p class="when"><b>When it pays:</b> the first month, on inquiries you already have.</p></div></li>
  <li><h3 class="tb"><button type="button" id="svb7" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="svp7"><span class="tn">07</span><span class="tw">Measure every dollar to the booked project</span><span class="th">Attribution to the booked project</span></button></h3><div class="tp" id="svp7" role="region" aria-labelledby="svb7" data-closed><p class="tph">How we fix it</p><p class="tpw">07 · Measure every dollar to the booked project</p><p>Every call and form tagged to its source and followed to sold or lost. Next month's remodeling marketing budget is decided by what booked, not by what clicked. <a class="tl" href="/attribution/">How we measure it <span class="ar">→</span></a></p><p class="when"><b>When it pays:</b> every month.</p></div></li>
 </ol>
 <p class="tlhint">Tap any step for what it covers.</p>
</div></section>

<!-- 4 MATH: table + calculator -->
<section class="soft" id="math"><div class="in">
 <p class="eb">What the marketing gap costs</p>
 <h2>The cheapest inquiry can be the <span class="o">most expensive way to win a remodeling&nbsp;project.</span></h2>
 <p class="lead">Your agency reports leads and clicks. You get paid when a project is booked and built, and the two can point in opposite directions.</p>
 <table class="cmp rpt">
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">The report says</th><th scope="col">The decision it drives</th><th scope="col">What's really happening</th><th scope="col">What we'd do</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">"We need more leads for spring."</th><td data-l="The decision it drives">Raise the advertising budget.</td><td data-l="What's really happening">New inquiries wait hours for a callback. The homeowner books the consult with the first remodeler who calls.</td><td data-l="What we'd do">Fix response speed and consult booking first, then decide whether more spend is needed.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">"Leads are up again."</th><td data-l="The decision it drives">Buy more marketing.</td><td data-l="What's really happening">Consults happen and proposals go out, but too few get signed.</td><td data-l="What we'd do">Review close rates, proposal follow-up and financing, and work the open proposals you already paid for.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">"Let's fill the calendar."</th><td data-l="The decision it drives">Spend more to generate demand.</td><td data-l="What's really happening">Crews and project managers are booked out for months.</td><td data-l="What we'd do">Weigh production capacity and margins. Aim demand at the most profitable project types or add capacity before scaling.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
 <div class="calc" id="calc">
  <h3>Same $250 inquiry. Three very different acquisition costs.</h3>
  <p class="sub">Every vendor sells you cost per lead. Cost per booked project is the number that decides your budget. Change the numbers to yours. Nothing you enter is&nbsp;saved.</p>
  <div class="chips" role="group" aria-label="Inquiry type"><button type="button" data-v="10" aria-pressed="false">Shared inquiry · 10%</button><button type="button" data-v="15" aria-pressed="false">Shared, reached first · 15%</button><button type="button" data-v="33" aria-pressed="true">Your own, reached in 5 minutes · 33%</button></div>
  <div class="cgrid">
   <div class="ins">
    <div><label for="rmCpl">Your cost per inquiry</label><output id="rmCplOut">$250</output><input type="range" id="rmCpl" min="50" max="800" step="1" value="250"></div>
    <div><label for="rmCr">Your close rate on those inquiries</label><output id="rmCrOut">33%</output><input type="range" id="rmCr" min="2" max="60" step="1" value="33"></div>
    <div><label for="rmJob">Average project</label><output id="rmJobOut">$26,941</output><input type="range" id="rmJob" min="5000" max="200000" step="1" value="26941"></div>
    <div><label for="rmGm">Your gross margin</label><output id="rmGmOut">29.9%</output><input type="range" id="rmGm" min="15" max="50" step="0.1" value="29.9"></div>
   </div>
   <div class="res" aria-live="polite">
    <p class="k">True cost per booked project</p>
    <p class="big"><span id="rmCpj">$758</span> <small id="rmOne">About 1 in 3 inquiries becomes a project.</small></p>
    <p class="gp" id="rmPct">9% of the $8,055 gross profit on a $26,941 project.</p>
    <p class="now" id="rmCmp">The same $250 inquiry as a typical shared lead at 10% costs $2,500 per booked project.</p>
   </div>
  </div>
  <details class="how"><summary>Sources and how it's calculated</summary><p>Cost per booked project = cost per inquiry ÷ close rate: $250 ÷ 10% = $2,500, $250 ÷ 15% = $1,667, and $250 ÷ 33% = $758. Search-ad inquiries for remodeling run about $150 to $400 depending on market and project type. Benchmarks published by contractor lead vendors and agencies (Housecall Pro, Minyona, Baadigi, 2025–2026) put shared inquiries at roughly 10–20% close and exclusive inquiries you generate yourself at 25–40%. Average kitchen remodel $26,941 and average bath $12,146 (Angi 2026, HomeAdvisor 2025); whole-home and additions run six figures. Share of gross profit = cost per booked project ÷ (average project × gross margin): $758 ÷ ($26,941 × 29.9%) = 9%; at a shared-lead 10% close, $2,500 ÷ $8,055 = 31%. The 29.9% default is the average gross margin for residential remodelers (NAHB Remodelers' Cost of Doing Business, fiscal 2024); set it to yours. Illustrative arithmetic, not a client result.</p></details>
 </div>
 <p class="calcnote">Read it down, not across. Cheaper inquiries move the number a little. Reaching a shared inquiry first helps. Owning the inquiry and reaching it in five minutes cuts the cost per booked project by two thirds, at the same price per inquiry. Then read it against gross profit: $1,000 to book a project is about 6% of the gross profit on a $60,000 addition and about 28% on a $12,146 bath.</p>
 <p class="midcta"><a class="btn" href="https://calendly.com/andrew-andrewryanmarketing/gap-review?utm_source=arm-site&amp;utm_medium=cta&amp;utm_campaign=site&amp;utm_content=rm-mid">Let's talk about your marketing&nbsp;<span class="ar">→</span></a><span>Bring your real numbers. We'll run them with you.</span></p>
</div></section>

<!-- 5 WHERE PROJECTS ARE WON OR LOST + GROWTH PATHS -->
<section class="light" id="leaks"><div class="in">
 <p class="eb">Where the project is won or lost</p>
 <h2>Most agencies report the first step. The project is decided in the last three.</h2>
 <p class="lead">Remodeling projects are won over weeks, not minutes. Homeowners compare portfolios, proposals, budgets, and timelines before choosing who gets the job. A marketing report that ends at the form fill sees none of that. We follow it to the booked project.</p>
 <ol class="path">
  <li><span class="st">1 · The search</span><b>Found isn't chosen</b><h3>"Kitchen remodeler near me"</h3><p>Your ad, map listing or project page has to win the click, then the inquiry.</p></li>
  <li><span class="st">2 · The inquiry</span><b>Reached in 5 minutes</b><h3>Before the third callback</h3><p>Reach a new inquiry inside five minutes and you are first onto the calendar. An hour later you are the third callback (Harvard Business Review, 2011).</p></li>
  <li><span class="st">3 · The consult</span><b>Where the sale is decided</b><h3>3+ bids, collected over weeks</h3><p>Homeowners choose on more than price. The consult, proposal, and follow-up build trust. We know great customer service sells.</p></li>
  <li><span class="st">4 · The build</span><b>The only number that pays&nbsp;you</b><h3>Booked and built</h3><p>Crews and project managers decide how many projects you can start a month, so marketing never outruns what you can build.</p></li>
 </ol>
 <h3 class="h3x">Where should your next million in projects come from?</h3>
 <div class="paths3">
  <div><p class="tag">Own your home market</p><h4>You're known in town. The bigger remodeler still gets the first call.</h4><p>Take the map pack, the reviews and the consult path in the towns you already build in.</p><a class="tl" href="/seo-for-remodeling-contractors/">See what it takes <span class="ar">→</span></a></div>
  <div><p class="tag">Open the next market</p><h4>New town, no reviews, no referrals.</h4><p>See the cost per booked project there before you hire a crew or open a design center.</p><a class="tl" href="/contractor-growth-marketing/">Model the next market <span class="ar">→</span></a></div>
  <div><p class="tag">Close more of what you run</p><h4>The consults happen, but too few sign.</h4><p>Fix the callback, the consult and the follow-up before you buy more demand.</p><a class="tl" href="/services/lead-to-booked-job/">Fix the second funnel <span class="ar">→</span></a></div>
 </div>
</div></section>

<!-- 6 PROOF -->
<section class="dark" id="proof"><div class="in">
 <p class="eb">The experience behind the work</p>
 <h2>Our founder built a sales department from scratch. Then made it run without him.</h2>
 <blockquote class="quote"><p>"Andrew was a huge help developing an in-depth marketing system for a window and door business we were working with. His process went way beyond a cookie cutter strategy. He took the time to learn about existing bottlenecks, how to identify new growth opportunities, and measure marketing effectiveness beyond traffic and leads. He even took into account the crew's installation capacity."</p><cite>Gregg Murray, Founder, Relaunch Digital · Verified Google review</cite></blockquote>
 <p class="prov">At a high-volume dealership, Andrew-Ryan Profaci built the sales department from scratch and ran it for 27 months: the phones, the follow-up, the close, and more than $34M in closed revenue. That's the work we do for remodelers. The method is what travels: response speed, show rate, close rate. We apply the same three numbers to remodeling consults, and leave you a marketing system that doesn't need you in every decision.</p>
 <div class="stats s4x"><div><b>11,564</b><span>Leads worked</span></div><div><b>2,135</b><span>Appointments shown</span></div><div><b>761</b><span>Sales closed</span></div><div><b class="o">35.6%</b><span>Shown appointments sold</span></div></div>
 <p class="travel">Andrew's own record from one dealership pipeline, reported monthly in writing. Not a remodeling client's results. The 35.6% is of appointments that showed, a different denominator from the inquiry close rates above. <a href="/about/">Read the full record&nbsp;<span class="ar">→</span></a></p>
</div></section>

<!-- 7 ENGAGEMENT -->
<section class="light" id="engagement"><div class="in">
 <p class="eb">How we work with remodelers</p>
 <h2>Three ways to buy remodeling marketing. Only one leaves you owning the engine.</h2>
 <table class="cmp">
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">What you're comparing</th><th scope="col">Lead marketplaces</th><th scope="col">A generalist marketing agency</th><th scope="col">Andrew Ryan Marketing</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">Who else gets the lead</th><td data-l="Lead marketplaces">Three or four other remodelers, at once.</td><td data-l="A generalist marketing agency">Often another client of theirs in your market.</td><td data-l="Andrew Ryan Marketing">Nobody. One remodeler per market, in writing.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">What gets measured</th><td data-l="Lead marketplaces">Leads delivered.</td><td data-l="A generalist marketing agency">Traffic, rankings, inquiries, and a monthly PDF.</td><td data-l="Andrew Ryan Marketing">Cost per booked project by channel, every month.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">When you spend more</th><td data-l="Lead marketplaces">Whenever you want more volume.</td><td data-l="A generalist marketing agency">When the agency recommends it.</td><td data-l="Andrew Ryan Marketing">When the numbers prove it, and after we check you have the capacity to build it.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">What you keep</th><td data-l="Lead marketplaces">Nothing. Stop paying, the marketing stops.</td><td data-l="A generalist marketing agency">Often not the ad accounts, tracking or data.</td><td data-l="Andrew Ryan Marketing">Your site, ad accounts, data and reporting, from day one.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
 <div class="notyet">
  <div>
   <h3>Sometimes the answer on more marketing is: not yet.</h3>
   <p>There is no honest percentage. Home remodeling marketing spend follows your cost per booked project and the stage you are in. Anyone quoting "5 to 10% of revenue" before asking your close rate is guessing with your money. In remodeling, capacity depends on more than available crews. Project managers, specialty trades, and scheduling all determine how much work you can take on. We account for those limits before recommending more demand. We will say so, and show you the math.</p>
   <p><b>Activity is not progress.</b> The budget follows what booked, stage by stage. <a class="tl" href="/pricing/">See every price range <span class="ar">→</span></a></p>
  </div>
  <figure class="season stages" aria-label="Where the next remodeling marketing dollar goes, by company stage">
   <p class="sth">Where the next dollar goes, by stage</p>
   <dl>
    <div><dt>Under $1M</dt><dd>Buy one predictable channel on top of referrals. Prove the cost per booked project, then stop guessing.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>$1M to $3M</dt><dd>Widen the channel that works and add a second. Lock down response speed and review velocity.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>$3M to $10M</dt><dd>Several channels reinforcing each other, tracked attribution, marketing that runs without your daily attention.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>$10M+</dt><dd>Predictable cost per booked project across markets. The question becomes which markets and crews earn the next dollar.</dd></div>
   </dl>
  </figure>
 </div>
 <div class="terms">
  <div><b>You own everything from day one</b>Website, ad accounts, data. Ad spend is paid directly to Google in accounts you control, never marked up.</div>
  <div><b>One remodeler per market</b>Defined in writing at signing, so you never compete with our other clients for the same homeowners.</div>
  <div><b>Terms that match the engagement</b>Smaller engagements start with a 3- to 6-month commitment, then run month to month. 12-month engagements carry a 90-day exit.</div>
  <div><b>You work with Andrew directly</b>No account managers between you and the person doing the work.</div>
 </div>
</div></section>

<!-- 8 FAQ -->
<section class="soft" id="faq"><div class="in">
 <p class="eb">Before you book</p>
 <h2>The questions remodelers actually ask about marketing</h2>
 <div class="faq">
  <details><summary>What is remodeling contractor marketing?</summary><div class="a">Remodeling contractor marketing, also called home remodeling marketing, is the system a remodeler uses to be found, chosen and booked by homeowners planning a kitchen, bath, addition or whole-home project. Built properly it covers search, ads, reviews, the in-home consult and the follow-up after it, and it is measured to the booked project, not to the lead or the click.</div></details>
  <details><summary>How do I choose a remodeling marketing agency?</summary><div class="a">Ask four things. Who owns the website and ad accounts if you leave (it should be you). How an inquiry is counted (a consultation request, not a click). Whether they can show you remodeling project-type pages that rank, not just a homepage. And how results are reported: ask for inquiries tied to booked projects. Whether it calls itself a remodeling marketing agency or a remodeler marketing company, an agency that will not put its price in writing is making a choice; ours is on the <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a>.</div></details>
  <details><summary>What is the difference between remodeling lead generation and remodeling marketing?</summary><div class="a">Lead generation is the part that creates demand: ranking, ads, exclusive inquiries, response speed. Remodeling marketing is the whole system that turns that demand into booked projects: it also covers the in-home consult, reviews and referrals, production capacity, and measuring every dollar to the booked job. More inquiries into a system that cannot convert or build them only creates a backlog. Where remodelers get their inquiries, and how to stop renting them, is on our page on <a href="/remodeling-contractor-lead-generation/">remodeling lead generation</a>.</div></details>
  <details><summary>What is the second funnel in remodeling marketing?</summary><div class="a">The first funnel gets the homeowner to reach out: SEO, Google Maps, ads and your website. The second funnel is everything after: how fast the inquiry is answered, whether the consult gets booked and kept, and whether the proposal is followed up and signed. Most remodeling marketing only works on the first. We measure and improve both, because a lost consult costs as much as an inquiry you never got.</div></details>
  <details><summary>How much should a remodeling company spend on marketing?</summary><div class="a">It depends on your close rate, your average project value and your stage. Under $1M, buy one predictable channel and prove the cost per booked project. From $1M to $3M, compound what works and add a second channel. At $3M and above, build several channels that reinforce each other. A percentage quoted before anyone asks your close rate is a guess.</div></details>
  <details><summary>What does a remodeling lead cost?</summary><div class="a">Search-ad inquiries for remodeling run about $150 to $400 depending on market and project type, with cost per click of roughly $8 to $35 for kitchen and bath terms depending on market, and higher for additions and whole-home work (agency benchmarks, BuiltRight Digital and Valley Marketing Group, 2026). Cost per lead is the wrong number to optimize. Cost per booked project, cost per inquiry divided by your close rate, decides whether remodeling marketing pays.</div></details>
  <details><summary>Why do shared leads from HomeAdvisor or Angi close so poorly for remodelers?</summary><div class="a">Because the same inquiry is sold to three or four contractors at once. The homeowner gets four calls and books with whoever reached them first. Benchmarks published by lead vendors and agencies put shared inquiries at roughly 10 to 20% close against 25 to 40% for exclusive ones you generate yourself. On a five-figure remodel that gap is the difference between a channel that pays and one that drains. We still use lead services where the numbers work, because a marketplace lead that closes at a profit is a good lead.</div></details>
  <details><summary>Google Ads or Local Services Ads for a remodeler?</summary><div class="a">Both, for different jobs. Local Services Ads cost per inquiry and favor remodelers with strong reviews and fast response; they work for bath and kitchen inquiries ready to book. Google Ads reach homeowners earlier in the research, including additions and whole-home work LSAs rarely show. We start with the one your reviews and calendar can support, then add the other.</div></details>
  <details><summary>How long until remodeling marketing produces booked projects?</summary><div class="a">Paid search can produce consults in the first weeks. Search rankings and reviews compound over three to six months. Response speed and follow-up improve the close rate on the inquiries you already have in the first month, which is usually where we start.</div></details>
  <details><summary>How do you find my remodeling marketing gap?</summary><div class="a">With the Booked-Job Model™, in three steps. First, your real numbers: inquiries, consults, design agreements if you sell them, booked projects, average project value, and how many projects your project managers and crews can run at once. Second, the one gap that costs the most: somewhere between being found and the booked project, one gap is capping growth, usually visibility, response speed, the consult, or production capacity. The others are noise until it is fixed. Third, fix that first, then scale what the numbers prove. When crews are the limit, more inquiries are a backlog, and the honest answer is "not yet." Want the quick version? The <a href="/6-gap-revenue-map/">6-Gap Revenue Map</a> runs in 60 seconds, no email.</div></details>
  <details><summary>What are the contract terms for remodeling marketing?</summary><div class="a">Terms depend on length and size. 12-month engagements have 90-day exits. Smaller engagements start as 3 to 6 month commitments, then run month to month. You own the website, Google Business Profile, ad accounts and all tracking data from day one, and we take one remodeling company per market, defined in writing at signing.</div></details>
  <details><summary>What does remodeling contractor marketing cost with Andrew Ryan Marketing?</summary><div class="a">A flat monthly fee by tier, never a per-lead price. Growth Catalyst, "We need a stronger foundation": $1,500 to $2,500 a month. Growth Engine, "We're getting leads. We need more jobs": $3,500 a month. Growth System, "We're ready to scale what's working": $5,500 to $7,500 a month. Growth Partnership, "We're growing across markets": $10,000+ a month. Every range is on the <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a>. Ad spend is separate, paid directly to Google in accounts you own, never marked up.</div></details>
 </div>
